{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And they drew near, etc.</b> The reason the verse says <b>and they drew near</b> — even though they were already standing before him to speak with him — is that perhaps they had distanced themselves of their own accord when Moses became angry with them and said to them ״קַמְתֶּם תַּחַת אֲבוֹתֵיכֶם תַּרְבּוּת אֲנָשִׁים חַטָּאִים״; therefore when they wished to speak, Scripture says ״and they drew near.״" "<b>Or</b> it may be that because Moses' words pinned the motive on something that was in their hands to correct, therefore they had boldness of heart and strengthened their weak hands to approach him further than the place where they had been when they spoke their first words to him." "<b>Folds for flocks, etc.</b> It is difficult why they needed this statement of ״folds for flocks״ and so on, for they should have said only the direct answer addressing Moses' objections, stated in their own words nearby — namely ״אֲנַחְנוּ נֵחָלֵץ חוּשִׁים״ and so on, ״לֹא נָשׁוּב״ and so on, ״עַד הִתְנַחֵל״. And it may be explained based on what Rambam wrote in chapter 11 of the Laws of Sales, and this is his language: one who sells a house to his fellow or gives it to him as a gift on condition that he go with him to Jerusalem on such-and-such a day, and this one took possession of the house — he acquires it when he goes with him to Jerusalem on that day. But if he stipulated and said to him ״If you go with me to Jerusalem on such-and-such a day, I will give you this house״ and so on, and he went with him on that day — even though he took possession of the house after fulfilling the condition, he has not acquired it, for this is an asmakhta (a conditional undertaking lacking binding force). Thus far. And in chapter 1 of the Laws of Sales he wrote, and this is his language: How is ḥazaka (an act of possession)? He sold him a house, he sold him a field, or he gave it and so on — once he locked or breached or fenced anything at all, provided that his actions accomplish something, he has acquired it. Thus far his language." "<b>And based on</b> these laws, the sons of Gad and the sons of Reuben were clever in how they framed their words, since what stood before them was a conditional matter involving a maṣṭe'ut (advance-grant) in its very essence, as Rambam wrote in chapter 11 of the Laws of Sales — namely, they accept the land on condition that they go up with them to the land of Israel. Therefore they put their acquisition of the land before the condition, which is the maṣṭe'ut that Rambam described, and said <b>folds for flocks we shall build</b> and so on — meaning, we shall take possession of the land for our acquisition from now. And the condition is the matter on condition that we go up to the land with our brothers on the day they go up. And they stated the manner of ḥazaka: <b>folds for flocks</b> and so on — they intended to perform ḥazaka in all the places through what they would do there, building folds for the flocks and cities for the little ones — meaning, they would build in a manner such that their actions accomplish something in the lands, according to the law of ḥazaka." "<b>And if</b> you will say: why did they need to say these things at all, when they could have taken possession on their own without any notification? Behold, Rambam wrote in chapter 1 of the Laws of Sales, and this is his language: In what case are these matters said? When he took possession in the presence of the seller or the giver; but when not in the presence of the seller and so on, he needs the seller to say to him ״Go, take possession and acquire,״ and afterward if he took possession he has acquired it, even though it was not in the presence of the owners. Thus far. And therefore they intended here to express the matter of ḥazaka before them, so that they would consent — without this, their ḥazaka is not a valid ḥazaka. And therefore when Moses agreed to the matter, he said to them <b>Build yourselves cities</b> and so on — which is exactly what Rambam wrote, that he said to him ״Go, take possession and acquire.״" "<b>And according to the reasoning</b> of the Ramban and his associates, who disagree with the Rambam on this law and hold that whenever the fulfillment of the condition depends on the recipient or the one receiving the gift, it does not carry the law of asmakhta — because the giver has fully resolved and transferred ownership — accordingly, one must find a reason for the statement about building pens and cities: it is so that they would not be obligated to cross the Jordan until they have prepared the pens and built the cities, and only then would they muster to go with them. And this is the meaning of the statement ״גִּדְרֹת צֹאן נִבְנֶה לְמִקְנֵנוּ וְעָרִים לְטַפֵּנוּ״, and afterward ״אֲנַחְנוּ נֵחָלֵץ חֻשִׁים״ etc."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:16:53.260023918Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}